id: design.graceful-shutdown
kind: design
title: Graceful shutdown
aliases: graceful shutdown, drain, connection draining, zero downtime
tags: operations, reliability
questions:
  - what is required for zero-downtime deployments?
  - how do you deploy a new version without dropping in-flight requests?
invariant: Stop accepting, finish in-flight work (idempotent or requeueable) within a deadline, then exit -- with clients retrying in the window where the endpoint list and the process disagree.
answer_30s: Drain on SIGTERM: flip readiness so no new traffic arrives, finish in-flight requests and messages under a bounded timeout, then exit -- and configure the platform's grace period longer than your drain.
answer_2m: |
  The reason it is subtle is timing: in Kubernetes the Pod is removed from
  endpoints asynchronously, so for a moment clients still hold the address of a
  process that has started shutting down. Clients need retries, and the server
  needs a drain window (and often a small preStop sleep) so the endpoints update
  before the listener closes. Workers have their own version: stop pulling from
  the queue, finish and acknowledge the current item, then exit; anything not
  finished must be requeued or idempotent. Two things to state explicitly in an
  interview: the grace period must exceed the drain timeout (otherwise SIGKILL
  interrupts work), and the same discipline is what makes rolling updates safe.
when_to_use:
  - every long-running process, especially behind an orchestrator or load balancer
minimal_example: |
  SIGTERM received
    -> readiness = not ready        (stop receiving new traffic)
    -> cancel background loops      (stop pulling work)
    -> await in-flight with timeout (drain)
    -> exit                         (before grace period expires)
failure_modes:
  - exiting immediately, so every deploy drops requests
  - drain timeout longer than terminationGracePeriodSeconds, so SIGKILL interrupts a write
  - no client retries, so the unavoidable window produces visible 5xx
  - workers that stop pulling but keep unacknowledged messages, so work is redelivered later
related:
  - k8s.deployment.rolling-update
  - k8s.probe.readiness
  - design.health-checking
  - rust.graceful-shutdown
  - rust.cancellation
  - recipe.graceful-shutdown
  - recipe.zero-downtime-deploy
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/pod-lifecycle/#pod-termination
